Why Are Dry Vacuum Pumps Critical to Semiconductor Manufacturing?

Vacuum pumps remove gases and maintain the pressure conditions required by semiconductor manufacturing equipment. Many fabrication processes require stable vacuum performance to control process chemistry, contamination and material deposition.

Dry vacuum pumps operate without using oil as the sealing medium in the process-gas pumping path. This design can reduce the risk of oil contamination and is suitable for applications where process cleanliness and compatibility with semiconductor manufacturing requirements are important.

The performance of these systems affects equipment reliability, maintenance requirements and fab productivity.

Advanced Wafer Fabrication Supports Market Growth

As semiconductor fabrication becomes more complex, manufacturers must manage increasingly demanding process conditions. Advanced device structures, additional process steps and specialized materials can increase requirements for vacuum stability and equipment reliability.

The market is also supported by investment in new fabrication capacity and upgrades to existing facilities. Each fab's equipment requirements vary according to its process technology, chamber configuration and production needs.

Dry vacuum pump suppliers must therefore offer products that meet process-specific performance, reliability and service requirements.

